This changes your active Kubernetes context from Docker Desktop to AKS. Verify it:  are you sure it happen on y local machine and not in Azure?

Yes. The context switch happens on your local Windows machine, but the selected context points to the Kubernetes cluster in Azure.

This command:

az aks get-credentials ...

downloads the AKS connection information and adds it to your local kubeconfig, normally:

C:\Users\User\.kube\config

Then:

kubectl config current-context

selects which cluster your local kubectl commands control.

So the flow is:

Local PowerShell
      ↓
Local kubectl + kubeconfig
      ↓
AKS API server in Azure
      ↓
Azure Kubernetes resources

After switching to AKS:

kubectl get pods

runs locally, but it retrieves Pods from Azure AKS—not Docker Desktop.

Your Docker Desktop context is not deleted. You can see all contexts:

kubectl config get-contexts

Switch back to Docker Desktop later:

kubectl config use-context docker-desktop

Switch to AKS:

kubectl config use-context aks-medical-api-dev

Always check before applying resources:

kubectl config current-context

That prevents accidentally deploying to the wrong cluster.

