////////////////////WHY CDR FAILED when we deployed cluster ///////

We can fix it declaratively with YAML, but we should use Argo CD’s official version-matched CRD rather than manually writing it:

kubectl apply --server-side --force-conflicts `
  -n argocd `
  -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/argoproj/argo-cd/stable/manifests/install.yaml

That installation YAML contains both the controllers and CRDs.

Correct. The issue was not caused by our Medical API Helm YAML or Argo CD Application YAML.

It was inside the Argo CD installation:

Our Application YAML          ✅ Correct
Our Helm chart                ✅ Correct
Medical API deployment        ✅ Running
Argo CD ApplicationSet CRD    ❌ Missing

Argo CD is a third-party Kubernetes tool. Its official installation manifest was supposed to install both:

argocd-applicationset-controller
applicationsets.argoproj.io CRD

The controller was installed, but its required CRD was not. This indicates a partial/incomplete Argo CD installation, not an application configuration error.

Also, we were not using ApplicationSet; we used an ordinary Application. Therefore, the Medical API still deployed successfully.

Next time we’ll install Argo CD with server-side apply, verify all three main CRDs, and only then create our Application:

Next time we’ll install Argo CD with server-side apply, verify all three main CRDs, and only then create our Application:

kubectl get crd applications.argoproj.io
kubectl get crd applicationsets.argoproj.io
kubectl get crd appprojects.argoproj.io

rgo CD adds this new resource type by installing a CRD.

Think of it like this:

CRD = definition/template of a new Kubernetes resource
CR  = actual resource created from that definition

For example:

applications.argoproj.io CRD
            ↓ allows Kubernetes to understand
kind: Application
            ↓
medical-api-aks Application

The same applies to ApplicationSet:

applicationsets.argoproj.io CRD
            ↓ allows
kind: ApplicationSet

In our cluster, the ApplicationSet controller tried to work with:

kind: ApplicationSet

but its CRD was missing. Kubernetes effectively answered:

I don't know what an ApplicationSet is.

That caused the controller to restart.

A simple OOP comparison:

CRD             ≈ class definition
Custom Resource ≈ object created from the class

Our Application CRD existed, which is why medical-api-aks worked. Only the unused ApplicationSet CRD was missing.
